  <learnings>["Refusing to accept missing evidence is what makes a standard real. Red-first was asked for four times before it arrived once; accepting the green suite instead would have reproduced the exact defect the audit existed to fix.", "Briefs should carry PRECEDENT, not just instructions. Citing that a previous builder stopped and reported rather than improvising is what licenses the next one to do it — permission to push back is only credible once someone has visibly used it and been backed.", "Specify the property that must NOT change, not only the defect to fix. Fail-open on a cache, deny-list posture on backups, byte-identical human path — a property to defend gives an agent a frame for judgement, which is what lets it catch its own regression mid-implementation.", "Name anti-patterns so they become shared vocabulary. Once 'declare your controls separately from red-first results' entered the briefs, every later agent did it unprompted, and the briefs got shorter while the bar got higher.", "Long multi-item agent runs truncate at the test-writing step, consistently — six times across this session. Tests-only resumes produced the best reports every time. Trade wall-clock for evidential completeness by scoping smaller.", "Apply the audit's own suspicion to the audit's remediation. The finding was 'the verification surface is decorative'; recursively applying it caught a brand-new vacuous test and a prune step that reintroduced the very cost it was removing."]</learnings>
